#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["httpx[socks]==0.28.1", "rich==15.0.0", "typer==0.27.2", "pyyaml==6.0.3"]
# ///
#MISE description="Lint PR bodies for the auto-apply host-apply declaration (marker line + named pending host changes)"
#USAGE flag "--pending" help="Generate paste-ready Auto-apply blocks for HOSTS (default: all auto-apply hosts) instead of linting PRs"
#USAGE arg "[value ...]" help="PR numbers to lint, or host names after --pending"
#USAGE flag "--json" help="Machine-readable JSON output in both modes"
"""Lint PR bodies for the auto-apply host-apply declaration.

Since eblume/blumeops#1559, merging any PR whose diff touches an auto-apply
host path (nixos/ringtail/**, darwin/indri/**) applies ALL of main at that
merge commit — including host changes merged to main since the last apply.
An agent PR touching those paths must say so, and name what else the merge
would bring. This task is the source of truth for which hosts are affected
and what is pending: it derives both, never a hardcoded path list.

Two modes:

- `--pending [host ...]` — print paste-ready PR-body blocks: the plain
  `Auto-apply: <host>` declaration line (deliberately not a `!!` pragma —
  that namespace is reserved for talos) plus the lint's list of pending
  host changes. Baseline = newest successful run of the host's workflow
  (Forgejo Actions run history); pending = first-parent merges since that
  baseline whose diff touched the host's trigger set (host globs plus the
  shared input files, e.g. forge/runners.nix). A generator, not a gate:
  exits 0 (unknown host names excepted).
- `<pr> [<pr> ...]` — check PR bodies for the declaration: a line
  `Auto-apply: <host>` (exactly one host per line) per affected host, and
  every pending item named as #NNNN or sha8 inside that host's blockquote
  block. No host paths touched → PASS. Exit 1 if any PR fails.

The derivation is fail-closed: a `class: auto` workflow must either be in
the NON_HOST_AUTO_WORKFLOWS allowlist (build-container — no host involved)
or list, in `on.push.paths`, a host glob `<nixos|darwin>/<host>/**` plus
optional plain shared input files. Anything else — broken YAML, a
string-form `on: push`, a non-list `paths`, an unexpected glob, the only
host glob cancelled by `paths-ignore`, a missing workflow file — exits 1
with a message. Since the PR's own version decides (workflows the PR
modifies, and the policy when the PR modifies it, are read at the PR head
sha), a PR that breaks its own workflow cannot bypass the check.
Fire-and-forget workflows (raw text contains `wait=false`) get the
"green means launched" verdict note (indri).

`main`'s full first-parent history lives in a tool-owned bare cache
clone of the canonical URL (~/.cache/blumeops-lint-auto-apply, overridable
via LINT_AUTO_APPLY_CACHE) — never a configured remote (some clones have
the canonical repo at `origin`, not `upstream`), and never the session
worktree, which the pod clones shallow and shares across sessions. In
check mode a failed fetch, or a baseline that is not an ancestor of main,
is itself a verdict (exit 1): a stale or missing main under-counts the
pending list → a silent false PASS.

Also runs as the `Lint / auto-apply` CI job (.forgejo/workflows/lint.yaml)
against every PR, so human PRs and late merges cannot skip it.

Usage:
    mise run lint-auto-apply --pending
    mise run lint-auto-apply --pending ringtail
    mise run lint-auto-apply 1571
    mise run lint-auto-apply 1571 1578 --json
"""

from __future__ import annotations

